<definition>
 <title>Event</title>    
      <statement>
        <p>An event is a collection of outcomes in a sample space.

        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>Notice that a sample space S is a set, and an event is a subset of S. We will review some basics of sets and 
    see how they can be represented with Venn Diagrams. Our universal set is always our sample space in this context because 
    the sample space contains all possible outcomes. We will assume all sets and elements are in S. </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-emptyset">
    <title>Empty or Null Set</title>
       <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\emptyset</m> denotes the empty or null set (the set containing no elements). 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
  <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>A \subset</m> B indicates A is a subset of B. Draw and shade set A so it is a subset of B.
        </p>
        <image width="40%">
  <latex-image>\begin{tikzpicture}[fill=gray, scale = 0.5]
\draw (0,0) circle (1.5) (-.5,1.25)  node [font=\tiny,text=black,above] {$B$}
      (-2,-2) rectangle (3,2) node [font=\tiny,text=black,above] {$S$};
\end{tikzpicture} </latex-image>
          </image>
    </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>A \cup B</m> is the union of sets A and B. Elements in the union might be in A or in B or in both A and B. Shade <m> A \cup B. </m> 
        </p>
        <image width="40%">
          <latex-image>\begin{tikzpicture}[fill=gray, scale = 0.5]
\draw[black] (0,0) circle (1.5) (-1,1.25)  node [font=\tiny,text=black,above] {$A$}
      (2,0) circle (1.5) (3,1.25)  node [font=\tiny, text=black,above] {$B$};
\draw (-2,-2) rectangle (4,2) node [font=\tiny,text=black,above] {$S$};
\end{tikzpicture} </latex-image>
    </image>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
      <m> A \cap B </m> is the intersection of A and B. Elements in the intersection are in A and also in B. Shade <m> A \cap B </m>.  
      </p>
    <image width="40%">
      <latex-image>\begin{tikzpicture}[fill=gray, scale = 0.5]
\draw[black] (0,0) circle (1.5) (-1,1.25)  node [font=\tiny,text=black,above] {$A$}
      (2,0) circle (1.5) (3,1.25)  node [font=\tiny, text=black,above] {$B$};
\draw (-2,-2) rectangle (4,2) node [font=\tiny,text=black,above] {$S$};
\end{tikzpicture} </latex-image>
          </image>
    </statement>
      </exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
  <p> A' is the complement of A (all elements in S that are NOT in A). Shade A'.
  </p>
    <image width="40%">
  <latex-image>\begin{tikzpicture}[fill=gray, scale = 0.5]
\draw (0,0) circle (1.5) (-.5,1.25)  node [font=\tiny,text=black,above] {$A$}
      (-2,-2) rectangle (3,2) node [font=\tiny,text=black,above] {$S$};
\end{tikzpicture} </latex-image>
          </image>
          </statement>
          </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are mutually exclusive or disjoint if <m>A \cap B = \emptyset </m>. Make a sketch.
      </p>
      <image width="40%">
  <latex-image>\begin{tikzpicture}[fill=gray, scale = 0.5]
\draw (-2,-2) rectangle (4,2)node [font=\tiny,text=black,above] {$S$};
\end{tikzpicture} </latex-image>
          </image>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A and B are exhaustive if <m> A \cup B =S,</m> Make a sketch.
      </p>
       <image width="40%">
  <latex-image>\begin{tikzpicture}[fill=gray, scale = 0.5]
\draw (-2,-2) rectangle (4,2)node [font=\tiny,text=black,above] {$S$};
\end{tikzpicture} </latex-image>
          </image>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
<p>
  These definitions extend to larger numbers of subsets. For example, given <m> A_1,A_2,...,A_k \subseteq S</m> 
  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
         <m>A_1, A_2,...,A_k</m> are mutually exclusive events if <m>A_i \cap A_j = \emptyset</m> for <m>i \neq j</m> (they are pairwise disjoint).
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>A_1, A_2,...,A_k</m> are exhaustive events if <m>A_1 \cup A_2 \cup \cdots \cup A_k=S</m>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>
  </worksheet>
